// REJECTS: Unsupported join type 'LEFT'. The supported join types are: [INNER, OUTER]
//
// The optional `(...)` prefix on a join pointer is a join TYPE, and the grammar accepts any
// identifier there -- `(PAREN_OPEN identifier PAREN_CLOSE)? joinPointer`. The closed set
// {INNER, OUTER} is checked in the walker, and it is checked twice: once on the head of a
// join sequence and once on each `>` continuation.
//
// LEFT is the join type every SQL author reaches for first, so a rewrite that stores
// whatever identifier it finds -- which is what the grammar tells it to do -- accepts a
// model legend-engine rejects, and the difference only appears when generation runs.
###Relational
Database fixture::neg::JoinTypeLeft
(
   Table T (ID VARCHAR(32) PRIMARY KEY, U_ID VARCHAR(32))
   Table U (ID VARCHAR(32) PRIMARY KEY, VAL VARCHAR(32))
   Join TU(T.U_ID = U.ID)
   View V
   (
      id: T.ID PRIMARY KEY,
      val: (LEFT)@TU | U.VAL
   )
)
